This guide describes how changes reach the `main` branch: how the
development environment is set up, how branches are named, how commits are
written, how pull requests are reviewed and merged, and which rules keep
secrets out of the repository. These rules are applied by discipline and by
local tooling (pre-commit hooks), not by GitHub branch protection.

## Getting started

<!-- Customise: add any extra setup this project needs, for example
environment variables to define in `.env` or services to start. -->

The project uses [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) to manage Python and its
dependencies, and [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) to check every
commit locally.

1. **Get the repository.**

   - Right after generating the project with Copier, turn its folder into
     a Git repository whose first branch is `main`:

     ```bash
     git init -b main
     ```

   - To work on an existing repository, clone it and move into its folder:

     ```bash
     git clone <repository-url>
     cd <repository-folder>
     ```

2. **Install the dependencies**, development tools included:

   ```bash
   uv sync
   ```

3. **Install the Git hooks.** Hooks are not stored in the repository, so
   this step is required once in every new repository and every new clone:

   ```bash
   uv run pre-commit install
   ```

   It installs every hook declared in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`: the code
   checks, run before each commit, and the commit message check. Without
   this step, nothing is checked when committing.

4. **Check the setup** by running every hook on every tracked file. In a
   freshly generated project, track the files first with `git add .`:

   ```bash
   uv run pre-commit run --all-files
   ```

## Branches

<!-- Customise: add branch types this project needs, or a naming rule
such as prefixing the subject with an issue number (feat/42-csv-export). -->

`main` always reflects a working state of the project. Nothing is committed
to it directly: every change goes through a branch and a pull request.

- **One branch per subject.** A branch carries a single change, so that its
  pull request can be reviewed and understood on its own.
- **Always start from an up-to-date `main`:**

  ```bash
  git switch main
  git pull
  git switch -c <type>/<subject>
  ```

- **Name branches `<type>/<subject>`**, where `<type>` is a Conventional
  Commits type and `<subject>` is a short, lowercase, hyphenated
  description. The most common types are:

  | Type       | Used for                                 | Example                  |
  | ---------- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
  | `feat`     | A new feature                            | `feat/csv-export`        |
  | `fix`      | A bug fix                                | `fix/date-parsing`       |
  | `docs`     | Documentation only                       | `docs/usage-examples`    |
  | `chore`    | Tooling and maintenance                  | `chore/update-tooling`   |
  | `ci`       | Continuous integration                   | `ci/test-workflow`       |
  | `refactor` | Code changes that do not alter behaviour | `refactor/config-loading`|
  | `test`     | Adding or fixing tests                   | `test/parser-edge-cases` |

  The commit message hook also accepts `build`, `perf`, `revert` and
  `style`.

- **Delete the branch once merged.** The local branch is deleted by hand,
  and `git fetch --prune` clears the outdated references to remote
  branches:

  ```bash
  git branch -d <type>/<subject>
  git fetch --prune
  ```

  On GitHub, enabling *Automatically delete head branches* in the
  repository settings deletes the remote branch after each merge.
  Otherwise, delete it with `git push origin --delete <type>/<subject>`.

## Commit messages

<!-- Customise: list the scopes this project uses, so that everyone picks
the same names for the same parts of the code. -->

Commit messages follow the
[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/)
specification:

    <type>(<optional scope>): <description>

- **Type:** one of the types listed under *Branches* above.
- **Scope (optional):** the part of the project affected, for example
  `cli`, `config` or `tests`.
- **Description:** in the imperative mood, lowercase, without a final
  period, and short enough to keep the first line under 72 characters.
  It completes the sentence "If applied, this commit will...".

Examples:

    feat(cli): add the export command
    fix(config): read the output path from the environment
    docs: add usage examples to the readme

A **body** can follow after a blank line to explain *why* the change was
made, when the description alone is not enough.

Each commit holds one logical change. A commit may be amended
(`git commit --amend`) only while it has not been pushed: rewriting a
pushed commit would rewrite history that GitHub already holds.

The format is checked automatically: a pre-commit hook running at the
`commit-msg` stage rejects any message that does not follow it.

## Pull requests

<!-- Customise: add a pull request template in
`.github/pull_request_template.md`, or require a review from another
contributor before merging. -->

Every branch reaches `main` through a pull request, even when working
alone: it gives each change a reviewable record, as if working in a team.

1. **Check the branch locally** before sharing it. Every test and every
   hook must pass:

       uv run pytest
       uv run pre-commit run --all-files

2. **Push the branch.** The first push links it to GitHub:

       git push -u origin <type>/<subject>

   Later pushes only need `git push`.

3. **Open the pull request** from the branch into `main`.
   - **Title:** a Conventional Commits message summarising the whole
     branch, for example `feat: add the csv export`.
   - **Description:** what the branch changes, why, and how it was
     tested.

4. **Review the changes** in the *Files changed* tab, as a reviewer would:
   read every line, look for leftovers (debug output, placeholder
   comments, unused code) and for anything listed under
   *Data protection rules* below.

5. **Merge only once every check passes.** If something fails, fix it
   with new commits on the same branch and push again.

## Merging

<!-- Customise: a project with many small pull requests may prefer
squash merges; update this section and the repository settings together. -->

Pull requests are merged with **Create a merge commit**, never squashed
or rebased.

- **Why:** every commit of the branch is kept as it was written, so the
  history shows how each change was built, commit by commit. The merge
  commit itself marks where a change ends, which keeps the history easy
  to read and makes a whole change easy to revert if needed.
- **One line per pull request:** the full history can be long, but
  following only the first parent of each merge gives one line per merged
  pull request:

      git log --first-parent --oneline main

- **Enforce it on GitHub:** in the repository settings, under
  *Pull Requests*, leave only *Allow merge commits* enabled, so that the
  other merge methods cannot be chosen by mistake.

After the merge:

1. Bring the local `main` up to date:

       git switch main
       git pull

2. Delete the branch, as described under *Branches*.

## Data protection rules

<!-- Customise: add the rules this project's data calls for, for example
where real input files are kept, or how test data must be anonymised. -->

The repository holds code, never secrets or real data. These rules apply
from the first commit, so that the repository can be made public at any
time without any clean-up.

1. **No secrets in the repository.** Connection strings, tokens and API
   keys live in `.env`, which Git ignores. A pre-commit hook running
   gitleaks blocks any commit that contains a secret. If a secret is ever
   committed, deleting it in a new commit is not enough, since it remains
   in the history: revoke it and generate a new one immediately.
2. **Invented test data only.** Tests and fixtures never reuse real data,
   even partially.

Before the repository is made public, its whole history is scanned for
secrets, not only its current files.
